/* ============================================================================
   goldbach-pair.epher -- a pair of primes that sums to an even n

   Goldbach's conjecture says every even integer greater than 2 is the sum
   of two primes; it is still unproved, but it has been checked for every
   even n up to 4 * 10^18 (Oliveira e Silva, Herzog and Pardi, 2014). This
   script finds the smallest such pair for a given even n: it tries the
   odd candidates p = 3, 5, 7, ... until p and n - p are both prime.

   Source: Goldbach's conjecture, letter to Euler, 1742; the check bound is
   from Oliveira e Silva et al., "Empirical verification of the even
   Goldbach conjecture", 2014. isprime is exact, so the pair found is a
   true Goldbach pair; the search depth is tiny for the values below.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/primes/goldbach-pair.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/primes/goldbach-pair.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   The one knob to edit is `n` below; it must be even and at least 6.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
const n = 100

/* ---- the search -----------------------------------------------------------------
   pair(n, p) returns the first odd candidate p with p and n - p prime.
   The recursion unwinds one level per candidate tried; for the small n
   here the first pair lies within a few steps (3 + 97 for n = 100). */
def pair(n, p) = if isprime(p) and isprime(n - p) then p else pair(n, p + 2)

// ---- results -------------------------------------------------------------------
print("smallest Goldbach pair for " + str(n) + ":", pair(n, 3), "+", n - pair(n, 3))
// The other three lines check the same search on smaller and larger
// evens; n = 98 needs the nineteenth odd candidate because 3, 5, ..., 17
// all leave composite partners.
print("for 50:", pair(50, 3), "+", 50 - pair(50, 3))
print("for 98:", pair(98, 3), "+", 98 - pair(98, 3))
print("for 1000:", pair(1000, 3), "+", 1000 - pair(1000, 3))
/* ---- expected output ----
= 100
= smallest Goldbach pair for 100: 3 + 97
= for 50: 3 + 47
= for 98: 19 + 79
= for 1000: 3 + 997
*/
